# The same refusal on the float side, and the same reason it is worth pinning:
# `to_float` takes bytes as happily as it takes a string, and said neither.
#
# A `none` is the argument because it renders without a module name, so the
# direct run and the run through an import say the same words.
#
# It arrives through a LAMBDA. The exhaustiveness check proves a none from a
# lenient index, a literal, or a call to a named group whose return set carries
# one; a lambda's call has no name to look up, so the check says nothing and
# the none reaches the runtime. That is the under-refusing direction and is
# safe, but it is also the only shape left that can pin this message: written
# as `measured 0` with `fn measured 0 -> none`, the check refuses the program
# before it runs.
import "std/text"

vanish = (_ -> none)

pub play = print "{text/to_float (vanish 0)}"
